{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-66002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-66002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-66002" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0eacd19b205682e2eab0f141e1332339fc322d25bc3ff346805b6ccd27eb19f1"} {"question" "q-66002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-66002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-66002" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/2e1f1ef6c82e30b5f9a591fca432914562f949a7ed5cabc3c4eb92bfecf899ee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0eacd19b205682e2eab0f141e1332339fc322d25bc3ff346805b6ccd27eb19f1"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-77805" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{8}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=(-38.682\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{38.682}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{38.682}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={321.318}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={321.318}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/f4ddf01db733d5ee2e73a4b8abb0bec84d9ee2f7be0fea03e99c112456be1c14.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{38.682}^{∘} \\\\  & ={218.682}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=321.318\\cdot 218.682≈70\\,266\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0eacd19b205682e2eab0f141e1332339fc322d25bc3ff346805b6ccd27eb19f1"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "62\\,236"] ["math" "65\\,571"] ["math" "68\\,547"] ["math" "70\\,266"] ["math" "76\\,234"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "70\\,266"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0eacd19b205682e2eab0f141e1332339fc322d25bc3ff346805b6ccd27eb19f1"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-120614" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-2,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-2 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{4}{5})≈-0.9273\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+2\\pi =-0.9273+2\\pi ≈5.3559\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.3559.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/d8951c588af989cc5a77b213f25a485b8a6e9db1d0f164fc326e65d40c0385c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & ≈4.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx≈4.1,\\,x≈5.4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0eacd19b205682e2eab0f141e1332339fc322d25bc3ff346805b6ccd27eb19f1"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"} {"question" "q-338723" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/state.json" "file_sha256" "3fea059b2135a024071be40766996c9b3218de48727812173f3d4a79f3b6bfe2"}] "usage" {"q-66002" []} "basis" 1504 "retractions" [[17592186115837 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\csc x = 3\\frac{1}{2}$\n2. $\\csc x = 1\\frac{1}{2}$\n3. $\\csc x = \\frac{1}{5}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186114394 17592186114395 17592186114396 17592186114397 17592186114398] "immutable_fields" [[17592186114393 :answer-field/correct] [17592186114393 :answer-field/id] [17592186114393 :answer-field/key] [17592186114393 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
